<p>I think you can make that work, but you should work on the narrative. Just explaining some properties and adding a log about how it got found won't make the cut, so I'd change the structure:<br />
- leave out most details from the actual description and focus on a log about the discovery.<br />
- try to focus on external input for the log(s) (e.g. in a found footage style), because if the foundation writes the logs, you have to do it in a clinical way, which would take away much tension. Maybe use interview logs or diary entries by the victim or whatever, this way you can build up narrative tension more deliberately, finishing with the conclusion about his room mate.</p>
<p>Anyway, that are just my 5 cent, I'm not experienced in crits, so take it with a small grain of salt.</p>

