						<a href="/forum/t-13945666/be-careful-what-you-wish-for">Be Careful What You Wish For</a>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4847335">
									

<p>(Can't give Greenlights)</p>
<p>This is a somewhat simple concept. I'm unsure whether it bears any resemblance to articles already on the site, but the idea of a laptop that can tell you when you're going to die feels a bit generic. That being said, the concept of exploring the existential and universal fear of death is something that definitely deserves to be developed and written.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;Here's&nbsp;some&nbsp;crit</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">-&nbsp;Begone&nbsp;crit</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<blockquote>
<p>SCP is a laptop that contains images of how it's user will die.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I don't see why this has to be some kind of everyday item. A strange artifact, technology, creature, plant, or anything that could simply convey images into the mind of an individual could be used instead. The physical design of an SCP is an important aspect for standing out in your reader's minds. There's limitless ways to be imaginative in this department, so don't be afraid to get creative!</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Too many subversions of the images will cause the user to enter a comatose state from which they cannot recover, and they cannot die. They are fully conscious in the coma, being able to see, hear, smell, feel, etc.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Why exactly? Wouldn't it eventually just end up showing them dying in a hospital bed if they avoided all other premature deaths?</p>
<p>It's an interesting idea, but it robs your narrative of a lot of its punch. Instead of having the researcher in your story simply fall into a coma, perhaps have him realize that death is truly inescapable. Or perhaps (this is a little generic I'll admit) have it so that the universe is trying to correct the imbalance caused by his avoidance of death. The research is then driven insane, either by the knowledge that death is unavoidable, or by the increasingly absurd ways in which the universe is trying to kill him. Maybe both.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Main narrative will follow a Junior Researcher fearing the death they see for themself. They become obsessed with avoiding death using the laptop and eventually go too far and have to face the consequences of never dying, but not really living.<br />
<br />
Hinting at the fate of the Researcher in the description and containment procedures, and seeing how the relatable fear of death can push someone to act in a way that they know is wrong.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I really enjoy this narrative, even if it is a little generic or perhaps played out. My suggestion would be to really focus on conveying the existential fear of death through wording in the various logs made by the researcher. If you end up getting greenlighted, I'd suggest contacting reviewers who specialize in this kind of writing when you post your draft.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>

