						<a href="/forum/t-13945666/be-careful-what-you-wish-for">Be Careful What You Wish For</a>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4847275">
									

<p><strong>Seeking Greenlights:</strong> Yes</p>
<p><strong>Page Type:</strong> SCP article</p>
<p><strong>Genre (Optional):</strong> Tragedy</p>
<p><strong>Page Layout (Optional):</strong> Standard SCP article with tests logs, emails, and security footage.</p>
<p><strong>Elevator Pitch:</strong> SCP is a laptop that contains images of how it's user will die. It's possible to avoid the death that the images show, but they will be replaced with the new eventual death of the user.(Example: A morbidly obese man may see images of himself dying of heart failure in a hospital. Exercising and losing weight would change his fate, and the images would show his death coming much later.) Too many subversions of the images will cause the user to enter a comatose state from which they cannot recover, and they cannot die. They are fully conscious in the coma, being able to see, hear, smell, feel, etc.</p>
<p><strong>Central Narrative:</strong> Main narrative will follow a Junior Researcher fearing the death they see for themself. They become obsessed with avoiding death using the laptop and eventually go too far and have to face the consequences of never dying, but not really living. Test logs and emails will show their growing obsession with using the SCP to avoid death, and surveillance log will show how that obsession culminates in unauthorized access to the SCP.</p>
<p><strong>Hook/Attention-Grabber:</strong> Hinting at the fate of the Researcher in the description and containment procedures, and seeing how the relatable fear of death can push someone to act in a way that they know is wrong.</p>
<p><strong>Additional Notes:</strong> I've struggled in the past with having a &quot;story&quot; in my ideas so I'm particularly interested if I'm more on track this time.</p>

							</div>

								
					
